{
  "image_name": "0e11777ec2f6607f81821d1864b26e1b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how epithelial gasdermins (GSDMs) can influence cancer in opposite ways depending on whether they are cleaved and activated. At the top, a tumor is shown arising within an epithelial layer, with zoomed cancer cells on either side illustrating two different states.\n\nOn the left, a cleaved GSDM in a cancer cell triggers pyroptosis, a lytic inflammatory form of cell death. This leads to release of inflammatory mediators and is linked to several consequences: chronic inflammation that can raise the risk of malignant transformation, direct death of neoplastic clones that lowers tumor burden, and broader effects on the tumor microenvironment that may be either antitumor or protumor. The diagram also points to specific pathways involving mediators such as HMGB1 and caspase- or granzyme-dependent cleavage of different gasdermins.\n\nOn the right, a full-length, uncleaved GSDM is associated instead with tumor-promoting behaviors. Certain family members, especially GSDMB, GSDMC, and GSDME, appear to support proliferation, migration, and adhesion, collectively promoting tumor progression. A separate branch suggests possible release of IL-1β, emphasizing the ambiguous role of IL-1 family signaling in carcinogenesis.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that epithelial gasdermins can either suppress tumors through pyroptotic killing or promote cancer through inflammation and enhanced malignant traits.",
